Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The previous strategy overused broad symbol-level constrained decoding inside
// SQL and only applied weak semantic biasing. The observed failure pattern
// ("SELECT <id>") indicates premature stopping after a shallow projection,
// which is especially harmful on Spider where correct SQL usually requires
// continuing through FROM / JOIN / WHERE structure. This revision therefore
// switches to a more SQL-directed token-by-token strategy once inside the
// constrained span.
//
// The new strategy is simple and aggressive:
//   1. Outside the constrained span, use UnconstrainedChunk only to reach the
//      opening "<<", preserving speed.
//   2. Inside the constrained span, never use ConstrainedSymbol. Instead decode
//      one constrained token at a time so we can bias logits at every step.
//   3. Track lightweight context from the current SQL prefix: tokens after
//      SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP, ORDER, and whether key structural keywords
//      have already appeared.
//   4. Before each constrained token choice, boost parser-valid candidates that
//      likely advance a complete SQL query: FROM is strongly encouraged after a
//      non-empty SELECT list when FROM has not yet appeared; WHERE / GROUP /
//      ORDER are encouraged only after FROM exists; schema tokens already seen
//      in FROM or SELECT are softly reused; and eosToken is heavily penalized
//      while the query is still structurally incomplete.
//   5. Closing ">>" is still performed only when parser.IsCompletePrefix holds.
//
// This directly addresses the evaluation failure by making the decoder resist
// early termination and prefer continuing from SELECT into the mandatory or
// highly likely relational structure of Spider SQL. All committed constrained
// tokens still come from ConstrainedStep followed by AppendConstrainedToken, so
// parser validity is preserved by construction.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
//   In the unconstrained branch, UnconstrainedChunk either stays outside the
//   constrained span or stops exactly at the opening delimiter; when we enter
//   the span we set currentConstrainedOut := [], and parser.IsValidPrefix([])
//   holds by precondition. In the close branch we call CloseConstrainedSpan
//   only after checking parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut), and the
//   returned state is outside the span so the implication is vacuous. In the
//   token-by-token constrained branch, all logit adjustments are only biases;
//   the actual committed token is obtained from ConstrainedStep, which returns a
//   parser-valid next token or eosToken. If it is not eosToken, AppendConstrainedToken
//   preserves parser validity of currentConstrainedOut.
// suffix:
//   In the unconstrained branch, while outside the span the implication is
//   vacuous; when stoppedOpen is true we set currentConstrainedOut := [], whose
//   empty suffix matches generated. In the close branch, CloseConstrainedSpan
//   resets currentConstrainedOut to [], so the implication is vacuous after the
//   update. In the constrained token branch, AppendConstrainedToken appends the
//   same chosen token to generated and currentConstrainedOut, preserving that
//   generated's suffix of length |currentConstrainedOut| equals currentConstrainedOut.
// progress:
//   UnconstrainedChunk appends exactly stepsUsed tokens and we set
//   steps := steps + stepsUsed, so |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps is
//   preserved by linear arithmetic. CloseConstrainedSpan appends exactly one
//   token and we increment steps by 1. ConstrainedStep consumes one decoding
//   step; if it returns eosToken we break after incrementing steps by 1 without
//   changing generated, and if it returns a normal token then AppendConstrainedToken
//   appends exactly one token and the same +1 step preserves the bound.
// cost accounting:
//   The loop keeps cost == 0 and uses steps as the returned cost. Every helper
//   that bumps helper cost is mirrored in steps: UnconstrainedChunk contributes
//   +stepsUsed, CloseConstrainedSpan contributes +1, and ConstrainedStep
//   contributes +1. Pure query/bias helpers do not affect steps. Therefore
//   assigning cost := steps at the end yields cost <= maxSteps.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END
generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

