Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The evaluation indicates the prior strategy achieved perfect grammar validity
// but very poor exact-match SQL quality. The failure preview shows the core
// issue: once inside the constrained span, token-by-token decoding with broad
// keyword boosting produced locally valid but globally incoherent SQL, often
// overextending after a complete query and drifting into extra identifiers or
// punctuation before the closing delimiter.
//
// This revision therefore changes the constrained policy substantially.
// Instead of manually biasing logits and then sampling one token at a time with
// ConstrainedStep, it uses ConstrainedSymbol as the primary decoding unit inside
// the SQL span. That helper lets the parser consume a coherent grammar symbol
// under a bounded token budget, which is better aligned with SQL structure than
// raw tokenwise decoding. Just as importantly, the strategy now checks
// parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) both before entering a
// constrained-symbol step and immediately after each successful append; if the
// prefix is complete, it closes the constrained span on the next iteration
// rather than continuing to generate more SQL. This directly targets the
// observed overgeneration like "... FROM <id>>><id> DESC ...".
//
// Outside the constrained span, the strategy still uses UnconstrainedChunk to
// efficiently reach the opening delimiter while preserving the required << >>
// framing. Inside the span, the strategy is intentionally simple: decode one
// parser-guided symbol at a time, append it, and stop as soon as the SQL prefix
// is complete. This removes the previous heuristic clause boosting that likely
// distorted model preferences on Spider schemas and questions.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the unconstrained-chunk branch, either we remain outside
//   constrained mode, making the implication vacuous, or we stop on "<<", enter
//   constrained mode, and set currentConstrainedOut := [], which is valid by
//   the method precondition parser.IsValidPrefix([]). In the complete-prefix
//   branch, CloseConstrainedSpan sets insideConstrainedOut to false, so the
//   implication is vacuous. In the constrained-symbol branch, ConstrainedSymbol
//   is called on a valid prefix; if it reports hitEos we break with unchanged
//   constrained state, and otherwise AppendConstrainedToken extends the valid
//   prefix with the returned parser-approved symbol, preserving validity.
//
// suffix: In the unconstrained-chunk branch, if we stay outside constrained mode
//   the implication is vacuous; if we open a span, currentConstrainedOut is []
//   so generated's empty suffix equals it. In the complete-prefix branch,
//   CloseConstrainedSpan exits constrained mode, so the implication is vacuous.
//   In the constrained-symbol branch, if hitEos we break without changing state;
//   otherwise AppendConstrainedToken appends exactly currentOut to generated and
//   sets currentConstrainedOut to that appended constrained content, preserving
//   that currentConstrainedOut is the suffix of generated.
//
// progress: UnconstrainedChunk appends exactly stepsUsed tokens and we increase
//   steps by stepsUsed. CloseConstrainedSpan appends exactly 1 token and we
//   increase steps by 1. ConstrainedSymbol consumes exactly stepsUsed decoding
//   steps; if hitEos it appends no generated token and we still increase steps
//   by stepsUsed, so |generated| remains within the larger bound
//   |generatedPrefix| + steps; if it returns a nonempty currentOut extension,
//   AppendConstrainedToken appends exactly |currentOut| - |old current| tokens,
//   and ConstrainedSymbol's stepsUsed accounts for the generated symbol, so the
//   linear bound is preserved by increasing steps by stepsUsed. Every branch
